body:
Could we make the reporting of this optional, or at least not an error? I don't mind doing the PR.

https://github.com/emscripten-core/emscripten/blob/ff229dc6a296cea250308ae4f415592a365bf1bc/src/library_browser.js#L1258

My use case is: depending on the browser, when running using RAF the main loop doesn't get called _at all_ when the tab is in the background. For drawing this is preferable, but there are cases where we'd still like updates. Our workaround is on a visibility change switch from `EM_TIMING_RAF` to `EM_TIMING_SETTIMEOUT`, which will get called once per second. This works great, only the console gets the "Looks like you are rendering without using requestAnimationFrame for the main loop" on every switch.
```
static EM_BOOL visibilityChange(int, const EmscriptenVisibilityChangeEvent* e, void*) {
    if (e->hidden) {
        emscripten_set_main_loop_timing(EM_TIMING_SETTIMEOUT, 60);
    } else {
        emscripten_set_main_loop_timing(EM_TIMING_RAF,         1);
    }
    return EM_TRUE;
}
```
Wouldn't `warnOnce()` be enough, or at least only giving the error when `ASSERTIONS` are enabled?

**EDIT: the idea of hooking into the visibility event to toggle between RAF and timeout is flawed (see post further down).**

comments:
[sbc100]: That fact that you are not actually rendering, but this warning still occurs seems like the problem.  I wonder if we can fix the "you are rendering" detection to be more accurate.

---

[cwoffenden]: Adding a simple `!document.hidden` stops the error when not drawing, deferring until the document is shown again.

---

[juj]: Instead of continuously toggling the timing mode, how about

```c
double lastRenderTime = 0;

void renderFromBackground(void*) {
  if (emscripten_get_now() - lastRenderTime > 900) {
    render();
  }
}

void render() {
  lastRenderTime = emscripten_get_now();
}

int main() {
   emscripten_set_main_loop(render, 0, 0);
   emscripten_set_interval(renderFromBackground, 1000, 0);
}
```
That way there is no need for registering to a visibilitychange event callback, and the approach is "stateless", so there is no danger of the main loop timing mode getting out of date, or event callback handling falling through.

---

[cwoffenden]: That'll work around it for sure, thanks.

I still think the PR is worthwhile since it doesn't error unnecessarily, then will once the page is visible again.

---

[juj]: I think a better fix is to change the warning to be more precise about what it is warning against. Posted PR #10062 that does that.

---

[cwoffenden]: The idea that prompted the post, switching between RAF and timeout for the main loop, is flawed, since browsers that don't complete the RAF until the tab is foregrounded again won't ever switch to running with a timeout (e.g. Chrome). The proposal a few comments up, [running a watchdog timeout](#issuecomment-566232729), is a better solution.

That said, even if the use case is flawed I still think it worthwhile only running the test with `ASSERTIONS` and using `warnOnce()`. See #10090.

body:
Given the test files:
```c
#include <emscripten.h>

int main() {
    EM_ASM({
        GROWABLE_HEAP_VIEW_I32(4, 8);
    });
    return 0;
}
```
and:
```html
<html>
    <head>
        <script src='a.out.js'></script>
    </head>
</html>
```

I get the error `RangeError: start offset of Int32Array should be a multiple of 4`.

By the looks of it, `TypedArray` view offsets and lengths are byte values, so the shifting in the `GROWABLE_HEAP_VIEW_*` functions need to be removed.

Switching from fastcomp backend to wasm backend, atomics begun to fail,
```c++
#include <emscripten/emscripten.h>
#include <emscripten/threading.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    uint8_t a = 1;
    uint8_t b = 2;
    uint8_t c = emscripten_atomic_exchange_u8(&a, b);
    printf("%d %d %d\n", a, b, c);
    return 0;
}
```

```
em++ a.cpp -pthread -s USE_PTHREADS=1 -std=c++14 -o a.html
```
Running a.html fails with
```
exception thrown: RuntimeError: unaligned memory access,@http://localhost:6931/a.wasm:wasm-function[100]:0x7194
@http://localhost:6931/a.wasm:wasm-function[27]:0xa18
@http://localhost:6931/a.wasm:wasm-function[28]:0xaa7
Module._main@http://localhost:6931/a.js:3415:32
callMain@http://localhost:6931/a.js:3881:28
doRun@http://localhost:6931/a.js:3943:31
run/<@http://localhost:6931/a.js:3954:7
a.html:1246:19
RuntimeError: unaligned memory access a.wasm:29076:1
```

Disassembling says that a `i32.atomic.rmw.cmpxchg`
```
   (i32.atomic.rmw.cmpxchg
    (i32.const 4544)
    (i32.const 0)
    (i32.const 1)
```
whereas it should have generated `i8.atomic.rmw.cmpxchg`? Did not try other atomics, I wonder if they might have similar issues?

comments:
[kripken]: Good find, that does look wrong... the code for these is
```cpp
uint8_t emscripten_atomic_exchange_u8(void /*uint8_t*/* addr, uint8_t newVal) {
  return __c11_atomic_exchange((_Atomic uintptr_t*)addr, newVal, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
}
uint16_t emscripten_atomic_exchange_u16(void /*uint16_t*/* addr, uint16_t newVal) {
  return __c11_atomic_exchange((_Atomic uintptr_t*)addr, newVal, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
}
```
And indeed it doesn't look like it says the underlying type edit: unless `newVal` is supposed to tell it?

It's hard to find docs on this - best I can see is https://releases.llvm.org/7.0.0/tools/clang/docs/LanguageExtensions.html - does anyone know how to do an atomic load of a certain size in c11? cc @tlively

---

[tlively]: The problem here is the cast of `addr` to `uintptr_t*`, since `uintptr_t` is an i32 and therefore is expected to be aligned to 4 bytes. Just get rid of that cast and everything should be good. Although there's no reason to take `addr` as a `void*` when it could be a `uint8_t*`, either.

Edit: Actually, the compiler needs complete type information here, so you must change the argument from `void*` to `uint8_t*`, etc.

[matthiasI]: I get the same error.
I think it is a problem with the generated JavaScript Code.
It wants to display another error but this does not work

The generated code looks like this:

```
  var UnboundTypeError=undefined;function throwUnboundTypeError(message, types) {
      var unboundTypes = [];
      var seen = {};
      function visit(type) {
          if (seen[type]) {
              return;
          }
          if (registeredTypes[type]) {
              return;
          }
          if (typeDependencies[type]) {
              typeDependencies[type].forEach(visit);
              return;
          }
          unboundTypes.push(type);
          seen[type] = true;
      }
      types.forEach(visit);

      throw new UnboundTypeError(message + ': ' + unboundTypes.map(getTypeName).join([', ']));
    }
```

It crashs at the last code line with "ReferenceError: getTypeName is not defined"

---

[zapfdk]: I know where the error comes from in my code, because I am trying to call the embinded function with the array as the function argument while the function requires a raw pointer. 
So I guess that is what the error message is trying to say, but can't because of a bug in the message code.

I finally just found a viable solution for my problem. The examples with pointers I found online all included calling the functions using ccall or wrapping the function using cwrap, but as I see this is not possible for member functions, or at least I don't know how to do this (so if someone knows, please let me know). 
What helped me the most was this blog post or whatever it is:
kapadia.github.io/emscripten/2013/09/13/emscripten-pointers-and-pointers.html
But instead of cwraping/ccalling the function I wrote a simple wrapper in C++, which takes an uintptr_t as the input parameter which is the address to the array. 
In the wrapper I am simply creating the float\* and make it point to the address. So it looks like this:

```
#include <stdint.h>

void ptrWrapper(uintptr_t address, unsigned int size)
{
        float *ptr = reinterpret_cast<float*>(address);
        testPtr(ptr, size);
}
```

testPtr is the actual function that takes the float\* as the argument. Size is the size of the block obviously.

In Javascript I am calling the function like in the example in the blog post.

```
pointerTest.ptrWrapper(dataHeap.byteOffset, blockSize);
```

pointerTest is just an instance of the PointerTest class that contains the wrapper function.

---

[froydnj]: @kripken, it looks like #3809 addressed this and #3487, but the automatic closing didn't work correctly once that pull request was merged.  Can you close those two issues manually?  Thanks!

body:
We are hitting a case where a large switch-case is autogenerated by a tool, and has a very simple pattern like follows

```
const char *foo(int x)
{
    switch(x)
    {
        case 1: return "a";
        case 2: return "b";
        ...
        default: return "";
    }
}
```

that has a lot of cases. When compiling this code, we are seeing two different issues pop up:
- Firefox aborts with "too much recursion" when running the generated code: https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1172895
- LLVM crashes by blowing up the call stack.
- Earlier in issue #781, a test was disabled since node.js crashes by blowing up the call stack.

Looking at the generated code, it becomes a huge nested if-else chain, and most parsers seem to be recursive in their handling of these blocks. Would we be able to retain a C/C++ switch-case, no matter how big, as a JS-side switch-case, if that would help avoid needing a linear amount of stack? That might also improve performance since it would not need a linear runtime with the if-elses?

Added a pull request with a new test #3533 to easily test this behavior.

This std::thread example fails during exit, an atexit looks invalid

```
// thread example
#include <iostream>       // std::cout
#include <thread>         // std::thread

void foo() 
{
  std::cout << "foo\n";
}

void bar(int x)
{
  std::cout << "bar " << x << "\n";
}

int main() 
{
  std::thread first (foo);     // spawn new thread that calls foo()
  std::thread second (bar, 123);  // spawn new thread that calls bar(0)

  std::cout << "main, foo and bar now execute concurrently...\n";

  first.join();                // pauses until first finishes
  second.join();               // pauses until second finishes

  std::cout << "foo and bar completed.\n";

  return 0;
}
```

Building with `-s USE_PTHREADS=1 -s PTHREAD_POOL_SIZE=2 -o a.html -std=c++11`

STR: Build the following file `a.cpp`

```
#include <xmmintrin.h>

void __attribute__((noinline)) foo(__m128 vec, __m128 &outLength)
{
    outLength = vec;
}

int main()
{
    __m128 x;
    foo(_mm_set1_ps(1.f), x);
}
```

with `em++ a.cpp -o a.html -O1 -g2`.

Observed: The generated output contains the following function

```
function __Z3fooDv4_fRS_($vec,$outLength) {
 $vec = SIMD_Float32x4_check($vec);
 $outLength = $outLength|0;
 var $outLength = 0, label = 0, sp = 0;
 sp = STACKTOP;
 $outLength = $outLength;SIMD_Float32x4_store(HEAPU8, $outLength, $vec);
 return;
}
```

and running the code gives the following error

```
TypeError: asm.js type error: local variable names must not restate argument names
```

Building Int64x2 utilizing code fails under optimization, when PNaCl ExpandI64.cpp attempts to operate on the vectors. Debug builds work ok.

As a result, the following SSE2 Int64x2 functions are currently not available in optimized builds and they are skipped in `test_sse2_full`:

```
_mm_add_epi64
_mm_mul_epu32
_mm_sub_epi64
_mm_cvtsd_si64
_mm_cvtsi128_si64
_mm_cvtsi64_sd
_mm_cvtsi64_si128
_mm_cvttsd_si64
_mm_move_epi64
_mm_sll_epi64
_mm_slli_epi64
_mm_srl_epi64
_mm_srli_epi64
_mm_storel_epi64
_mm_stream_si64
_mm_unpackhi_epi64
_mm_unpacklo_epi64
```

As a small test case, one can attempt to build the following with `-O1` or higher:

```
#include <emmintrin.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

__m128i set_64x2(int64_t a, int64_t b) { union { int64_t x[2]; __m128i m; } u; u.x[0] = a; u.x[1] = b; return u.m; }
int64_t get_64x2_lo(__m128i m) { union { int64_t x[2]; __m128i m; } u; u.m = m; return u.x[0]; }
int64_t get_64x2_hi(__m128i m) { union { int64_t x[2]; __m128i m; } u; u.m = m; return u.x[1]; }

int main()
{
    __m128i a = set_64x2(1, 2);
    __m128i b = set_64x2(3, 4);
    __m128i c = _mm_add_epi64(a, b);
    printf("%lld %lld\n", get_64x2_lo(c), get_64x2_hi(c));
}
```

which fails on `LLVM ERROR: I->getType() == I->getOperand(0)->getType()` here https://github.com/kripken/emscripten-fastcomp/blob/master/lib/Transforms/NaCl/ExpandI64.cpp#L831.

Some of the c++ standard library appears to unusable before main has been entered.
Printing a string seems to have no effect.
Printing a new line with `std::cout << std::endl;` causes an interger to be printed and then the program to terminate.

``` C++
#include <iostream>

class A {
public:
  A() {
    std::cout << "bug";
    //std::cout << std::endl;
  }
};

A a;

int main() {
  std::cout << "free code" << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```

When I compiled this with clang it outputs "bugfree code" and when I compiled it with emscripten it outputs "free code".

According to the link [here](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Workers_API/Using_web_workers#Passing_data_by_transferring_ownership_%28transferable_objects%29) it is possible to pass certain types of objects (transferable objects) to or from a web worker with ownership transfer(no copy), this can be done using [post messages](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Worker/postMessage) but since emscripten's implementation (library_browser.js) doesn’t seem to support this: [here](https://github.com/kripken/emscripten/blob/master/src/library_browser.js#L1345) and [here](https://github.com/kripken/emscripten/blob/master/src/library_browser.js#L1335), it is impossible with the current implementation.

I tried to modify the library for one of the functions to test if it would work at all

``` js
emscripten_worker_respond: function(data, size) {
    if (workerResponded) throw 'already responded with final response!';
    workerResponded = true;
    var respondMsg = new Uint8Array({{{ makeHEAPView('U8', 'data', 'data + size') }}}) ; // XXX copy to a new typed array as a workaround for chrome bug 169705
    var objectData = {
      'callbackId': workerCallbackId,
      'finalResponse': true,
      'data': data ? respondMsg : 0 
    };
    postMessage(objectData,[objectData.data.buffer]);
  }
```

I measured the memory consumption with / without the modification (because without transferring the ownership two copies are needed vs one copy needed in case of transfer the ownership)
and it seems to be working.

What is the reason that emscripten does not support this by default?

Thanks.

comments:
[kripken]: Thanks for reporting this. It would be good to fix it properly: In a non-minified build, do you see which line is failing? That should tell us which specific WebAudio method is not working ok in Safari.

---

[Dimitory]: Oh, sorry, Ive forgot it.

```
pauseWebAudio: function(audio) {
      if (!audio) return;
      if (audio.webAudioNode) {
        try {
          // Remember where we left off, so that if/when we resume, we can restart the playback at a proper place.
          audio.currentPosition = (SDL.audioContext['currentTime'] - audio.startTime) % audio.resource.webAudio.decodedBuffer.duration;
          // Important: When we reach here, the audio playback is stopped by the user. But when calling .stop() below, the Web Audio
          // graph will send the onended signal, but we don't want to process that, since pausing should not clear/destroy the audio
          // channel.
          audio.webAudioNode['onended'] = undefined;
          audio.webAudioNode.stop();                        <= exception gained there
          audio.webAudioNode = undefined;
        } catch(e) {
          Module.printErr('pauseWebAudio failed: ' + e);
        }
      }
      audio.paused = true;
    },
```

As I understood, `stop()` method should be replaced to `stop(0/*currentTime*/);` or `pause(); currentTime = 0;`

---

[kripken]: Thanks!

Ok, this looks like a WebKit bug, since that is [an optional parameter](https://dvcs.w3.org/hg/audio/raw-file/tip/webaudio/specification.html#AudioBufferSourceNode-section). Do you want to file a bug for them?

Meanwhile I'll add the `0` parameter as a workaround.

---

