Trying to upgrade to 1.36.12 I get error `Invalid argument` in `mkdirTree`:

``` js
    // Creates a whole directory tree chain if it doesn't yet exist
    mkdirTree: function(path, mode) {
      var dirs = path.split('/');
      var d = '';
      for (var i = 0; i < dirs.length; ++i) {
        d += '/' + dirs[i];
        try {
          FS.mkdir(d, mode);
        } catch(e) {
          if (e.errno != ERRNO_CODES.EEXIST) throw e; // <- Invalid argument
        }
      }
    },
```

Investigating it seems it doesn't like `throw e`. Replacing `throw e` with `{}` fixes the error and the page works. I would make a pull request but I don't know why this error happens.

Compiling the following with master `emcc`:
```c
#include <netdb.h>

int main () {
    void* thing = gethostbyname("google.com");
    return 0;
}
```
gives no compiler errors, but at link time fails with:

    warning: unresolved symbol: htons

From what I can see from the muslc library code, the callpath looks like: `gethostbyname` -> `gethostbyname2` -> `gethostbyname2_r` -> `getaddrinfo` -> our friend `htons`

A workaround is simply to force `htons` to be included at link time by referencing it pointlessly. Example program:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <netdb.h>

int main () {
    void* thing = gethostbyname("google.com");
    void* other = htons;
    return 0;
}
```

I am trying to write an Emscripten app that will display multiple images in different windows, which should be possible according to the SDL2 docs. I wrote a sample app which downloads 3 different images of ScarJo, using emscripten_wget, then i call a function which creates a window and renderer for each one. I cna see the images pop up on screen, one right after the other, but it appears that each subsequent call to SDL_CreateWindow erases the window that was there before it, rather than creating a new one. Also, any attempt to set a SDL_Window's x/y coordinates, either during construction, or a call to SDL_SetWindowPosition fail. The window stays centered in the browser. Here is the code.
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string.h>
#include <emscripten/fetch.h>
#include <emscripten/emscripten.h>
#include <SDL2/SDL.h>
#include <SDL2/SDL_image.h>
#include <assert.h>

void displayImage(const char* filename)
{
    static int width;
    static int height;
  std::cout <<"Download completed: " << filename << std::endl;

  SDL_Surface *image = IMG_Load(filename);
  if (!image)
  {
     printf("IMG_Load: %s\n", IMG_GetError());
     ;
  }
  assert(image->format->BitsPerPixel == 32);
  assert(image->format->BytesPerPixel == 4);
  assert(image->pitch == 4*image->w);

  SDL_Window *window = SDL_CreateWindow(filename, width, 0, image->w, image->h, 0);
  SDL_Renderer *renderer= SDL_CreateRenderer(window, -1, 0);
  //SDL_CreateWindowAndRenderer(image->w, image->h, 0, &window, &renderer);

  /**
   * Set up a white background
   */
  SDL_SetRenderDrawColor(renderer, 255, 255, 255, 255);
  SDL_RenderClear(renderer);

  SDL_Rect dest = {.x = 0, .y = 0, .w = image->w, .h = image->h};
  SDL_Texture *tex = SDL_CreateTextureFromSurface(renderer, image);
  SDL_RenderCopy (renderer, tex, NULL, &dest);
  //SDL_SetWindowPosition(window, width, 0);
  height += image->h;
  width += image->w;

  /**
   * Now that the image data is in the renderer, we can free the memory
   * used by the texture and the image surface
   */
  SDL_DestroyTexture (tex);
  SDL_FreeSurface (image);

  /**
   * Show what is in the renderer
   */
  SDL_RenderPresent(renderer);
}

void downloadFail(const char* filename)
{
    std::cout << "Unable to download file " << filename << std::endl;
    exit (2);
}

int main()
{

 EM_ASM(
      FS.mkdir('/working');
      FS.mount(MEMFS, {}, '/working');
    );

  SDL_Init(SDL_INIT_VIDEO);

  printf("Downloading...\n");

  //Download image and save it to the File System
  //emscripten_async_wget("https://pbs.twimg.com/media/CAoh21KWEAARhYx.png", "/working/Scarlett1.png", downloadSuccess, downloadFail);
  //emscripten_async_wget("http://static1.comicvine.com/uploads/original/11129/111299710/5451270-1446621456-b1d30.jpg", "/working/Scarlett2.png", downloadSuccess, downloadFail);
  //emscripten_async_wget("http://i.imgur.com/RNjRWB4.jpg", "/working/Scarlett3.png", downloadSuccess, downloadFail);

  const char* filename = "working/Scarlett1.png";
  emscripten_wget("https://pbs.twimg.com/media/CAoh21KWEAARhYx.png", filename);
  displayImage(filename);

  filename = "working/Scarlett2.jpg";
  emscripten_wget("http://static1.comicvine.com/uploads/original/11129/111299710/5451270-1446621456-b1d30.jpg", filename);
  displayImage(filename);

  filename = "working/Scarlett3.jpg";
  emscripten_wget("http://i.imgur.com/RNjRWB4.jpg", filename);
  displayImage(filename);

  return 0;
}
```
Is this a limitation of emscripten, or is there a way around this? I also tried some code I found online, a simple tutorial for creating a solid green square inside of a solid red one. The url is [http://stackoverflow.com/questions/21691450/how-to-create-a-subwindow-using-sdl](url)

No go. The green window replaces the red one, rather than being drawn inside of it? Can anyone think of what steps I am missing?

