comments:
[kripken]: "Flexible" here means that something can be either signed or unsigned. Like say the constant "0" - it's ok to add that to either a signed or an unsigned value. "nonsigned" means not having a sign, that is, it's not an integer (which is either signed or unsigned).

For `%`, I think it should be like `/`, which is "nonsigned": that way it is properly not signed or unsigned for a float operation, and if it's an integer operation, there will be a signed or unsigned coercion on the outside which enforces that.

For a testcase, if it's hard to get one it's fine to fix this without one. But I'd take a look at the intermediate JS to see what's going on - perhaps the '%' doesn't arrive from compiled code, or arrives in a weird way? Seeing the surroundings might help clarify.

---

[Beuc]: The immediate surroundings (`node`) are:
```
binary,%,name,$393,unary-prefix,+,num,2
```
(`$393` may change depending on the code, e.g. `$202`)

The actual intermediate file seems to be removed on error and I don't see any instance of '%' in my /tmp/*.js.
I thought it was working on Emterpreter code rather than JS code - I'll have to look at this deeper.

Note: for completeness, I have had this warning for weeks when working with Python:
```
emcc: warning: cannot represent a NaN literal '0x5564756ccf20' with custom bit pattern in NaN-canonicalizing JS engines (e.g. Firefox and Safari) without erasing bits!
  in   %. = select i1 %68, double 0x7FF8000000000000, double 0xFFF8000000000000 in _Py_parse_inf_or_nan()
```
or
```
emcc: warning: cannot represent a NaN literal '0x56378e61de20' with custom bit pattern in NaN-canonicalizing JS engines (e.g. Firefox and Safari) without erasing bits!
  in   %. = select i1 %68, double 0x7FF8000000000000, double 0xFFF8000000000000 in _Py_parse_inf_or_nan() 
```
(even though Py_parse_inf_or_nan has no '%' in its code)
It shouldn't be related but there are 2 suspicious '%' occurrences in the error message.

---

[kripken]: Looks like it's doing `$var % (+2)` which is very odd.

Try EMCC_DEBUG=2 in the env, it should save all temp JSes in the temp dir (`/tmp/emscripten_temp`). Or, you can add more debugging in the js optimizer near the line that throws, like printing the function name etc.

---

[kripken]: (Those other warnings from LLVM with `%. = select` etc. are just `%` being used as an LLVM prefix, that's coincidental and unrelated.)

---

