We'd been been having spurious invalid GL context related bugs which we found were caused by reloading the page (whereby the outgoing page's RAF callback was still getting executed after clean-up, throwing a JS exception and halting the currently loading page). A quick fix was to test for `emscripten_is_webgl_context_lost(NULL)` before drawing, which worked fine for our debug builds, but failed for release when using `--closure 1` (with `Module.ctx` having been renamed).

We added a workaround (in the native code with EM_JS) which fixed our issue in both debug and release:
```
EM_JS(bool, _emscripten_is_webgl_context_lost, (), {
    if (GL.currentContext && GL.currentContext.GLctx) {
        return GL.currentContext.GLctx.isContextLost();
    }
    return true;
});
```
This still has the same single context limitation as the original implementation. Following the pattern used in other `library_html5.js` functions a multi-context implementation looks to be:
```
EM_JS(bool, _emscripten_is_webgl_context_lost, (const char* target), {
    if (target) {
        target = JSEvents.findEventTarget(Pointer_stringify(target));
    } else {
        target = Module["canvas"];
    }
    if (target && target.GLctxObject) {
        return target.GLctxObject.GLctx.isContextLost();
    }
    return true;
});
```
This works (for us) but I have a feeling this should be added as a one-liner in `closure-externs.js`?

