[beakbeak]: I wrote a simple test case:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <emscripten/val.h>

int
main() {
    auto obj1 = emscripten::val::global("Uint8Array").new_(4);
    std::cout << "before memory growth" << std::endl;

    // force memory growth
    auto heapu8 = emscripten::val::global("Module")["HEAPU8"];
    delete[] new char[heapu8["byteLength"].as<size_t>() + 1];

    auto obj2 = emscripten::val::global("Uint8Array").new_(4);
    std::cout << "after memory growth" << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```
Compile like so: `em++ test.cpp -o test.html --bind -std=c++11 -s ALLOW_MEMORY_GROWTH=1`

The exception is thrown with `incoming` in Windows Chrome, Firefox and Edge, but not thrown after the patch. I tried adding it to the embind test suite, but for some reason couldn't reproduce the exception there, possibly because the old heap `ArrayBuffer` does not become detached after memory growth in Node.js.

---

[kripken]: Thanks for the testcase! Ok, I can reproduce the problem there, and yes, now I see that we are capturing the heap view incorrectly.

