[ShigekiKarita]: P.S. I think that optimizer's serialization can be similar to module's `register_parameter` way instead of hard coded serialization lines like
```c++
  // these two functions only calls `static void serialize(..)`
  void save(serialize::OutputArchive& archive) const override;
  void load(serialize::InputArchive& archive) override;

  template <typename Self, typename Archive>
  static void serialize(Self& self, Archive& archive) {
    TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE(step_buffers);
    TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE(exp_average_buffers);
    TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE(exp_average_sq_buffers);
    TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE(max_exp_average_sq_buffers);
 }
 // TORCH_OPTIM_SERIALIZE calls torch::optim::detail::serialize and assumes that the variable self is defined
```
This is little bit too nested and complex . I do not want to call `detail` namespace (even wrapped in that macro) when I define my own optimizer because it seems to be private conventionally. https://stackoverflow.com/questions/26546265/what-is-the-detail-namespace-commonly-used-for

---

[ShigekiKarita]: https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/fe15d6a2c231a7bc1b32781217ed336ccf9adff7/torch/csrc/api/include/torch/optim/serialize.h#L46-L60

I suspect this `emplace_back` just appends tensors to optimizer's buffers without `.clear()` existing tensors. This can result in the constant increasing of memory allocation. However, I do not have time to debug this now. Has anyone reproduced this memory leak yet?

---

[ezyang]: cc @goldsborough

---

[goldsborough]: https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/pull/15926 fixes the bug. I also moved the serialize functions out of the detail namespace. The macro is really a convenience for the library implementation. I've prefixed it with an underscore to make this clearer. You can just call the function yourself or define your own macro.

The `register_parameter` approach might be viable although optimizers don't just have one type of buffer, but tensor buffers, int buffers, float buffers which would each need to be stored separately. I think a registration-based API would be a little easier on users, but defining your own optimizers is rare enough that I wouldn't rewrite the whole API for this right now.

---

[ShigekiKarita]: Thanks for the fix. I agree with your thoughts on the API.

body:
# Introduction

Unrolling and vectorized memory access is beneficial for bandwidth bound kernels, and it is becoming more important. We need unrolling and vectorized memory access to achieve great performance.

# Current status of gpu loop

The current design of gpu loop (simplified) works as follows:

```c++
template<int nt, int vt, typename func_t>
__global__ void elementwise_kernel(int N, func_t f) {
  int tid = threadIdx.x;
  int nv = nt * vt;
  int idx = nv * blockIdx.x + tid;
  #pragma unroll
  for (int i = 0; i < vt; i++) {
    if (idx < N) {
      // inside f, the address of data is computed, the data is loaded,
      // the computation is done and data is stored back to memory.
      f(idx);
      idx += nt;
    }
  }
}

void gpu_kernel(TensorIterator& iter, const func_t& f) {
  elementwise_kernel<nt, vt, func_t><<<grid, block, 0, stream>>>(N, [=]GPU_LAMBDA(int idx) {
    arg0_t* out = (arg0_t*)(data[0] + strides[0] * idx);
    *out = f(data[1] + strides[1] * idx, data[2] + strides[2] * idx, ...);
  });
}

void add_kernel_cuda(TensorIterator& iter) {
  gpu_kernel(iter, []GPU_LAMBDA(thrust_t a, thrust_t b) -> thrust_t {
    return a + b;
  });
}
```

The schedule is controlled by `elementwise_kernel` while all the memory access is done inside a lambda that `elementwise_kernel` have no knowledge about. And inside the lambda, it does the fetch, compute and store of only a single element. Unfortunately, this design is not very friendly to implementing vectorized load. I am planning to refactor it as the following while doing the work:

```c++
__global__ void elementwise_kernel(func_t f, data, strides) {
  #pragma unroll
  compute addresses

  #pragma unroll
  data loading, maybe vectorize

  #pragma unroll
  compute f(...)

  #pragma unroll
  store results
}
```

this new design would allow us to easily control data loading and storing.

# The plan

- [x] **Step 0**: Move old code to a new namespace `legacy`, and all new code will go to `modern`. These two namespaces will coexist for a while and eventually, both of them will go away as we merge them. (#31974)
- [x] **Step 1**: Write new codes for the case of trivial 1d contiguous problem, supporting unroll. (#31974)
- [x] **Step 2**: Refactor the code in step 1 to do vectorized load and store (#32383).
- [x] **Step 3**: Get rid of unnecessary dynamic casting (#32755).
- [x] **Step 4**: Legacy code cleanup -- `gpu_kernel_with_index` (#32777).
- [x] **Step 5**: Migrate `torch.where` to TensorIterator (#32984).
- [x] **Step 6**: Support the case where arguments do not have the same dtype (#33553).
- [x] **Step 7**: Remove `gpu_kernel_with_index` (#33370).
- [x] **Step 8**: Template argument cleanup (#33308).
- [ ] **Step 9**: Make ELU great again (#33244).
- [ ] **Step ..N**: Not planned in detail yet 

cc @VitalyFedyunin @ngimel @mruberry

