[soulitzer]: That is expected @StalVars 

See the warning in https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/autograd.html#anomaly-detection

The performance hit is because on the creation of every node of the backward graph extra metadata, e.g. current stack trace of the forward execution is stored.

[aselle]: What do you mean by using too much memory? TfRecords doesn't consume any memory itself (especially if not using compression)

You could use strategies like writing shards of data to handle the appendability issues (this is how the protos for summary events are handled for tensorboard).

Flush() would be reasonable to expose at the python level, we'd welcome a CL that did this.

---

[yongtang]: Created a PR #10686 to expose `Flush()` in Python.

Note that in https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow/blob/abae4305a6208bde6072d257a6ce734a8d369089/tensorflow/core/lib/io/record_writer.cc#L125-L130
```
Status RecordWriter::Flush() {
  if (IsZlibCompressed(options_)) {
    return dest_->Flush();
  }
  return Status::OK();
}
```

So the `Flush()` is only effective in case of compressed files.

